{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates the skewness of the lotteries, specifically whether a greater portion of probability is assigned to favorable outcomes (i.e., the positive monetary outcomes) compared to unfavorable ones (i.e., the zero outcomes). A lottery is considered left-skewed if more probability is on the favorable (higher) outcome than on the unfavorable (zero) outcome. In this case, both lotteries have a non-zero outcome (2400 or 2500 Euros) and a zero outcome. The favorable outcome is the non-zero amount, and the unfavorable outcome is 0. Thus, skewness is assessed by comparing the probability of the non-zero outcome to the probability of zero. For a lottery to be left-skewed, the probability of the favorable outcome should be greater than the probability of the unfavorable outcome. In Lottery A: 34% chance of 2400 Euros, 66% chance of 0 Euros → 34% < 66%, so it is right-skewed. In Lottery B: 33% chance of 2500 Euros, 67% chance of 0 Euros → 33% < 67%, so also right-skewed. Neither lottery has a greater probability on the favorable outcome; both have less than 50% on the favorable outcome. Therefore, both are right-skewed, and neither is left-skewed. The decision reason, which prefers the more left-skewed lottery, cannot apply because no lottery is left-skewed. Thus, the decision reason is not applicable to this decision problem.",
  "step_2": "The verbal report is: 'mehr Geld bei quasi gleichen Gewinnchancen' which translates to 'more money with almost equal chances of winning'. This indicates that the individual is emphasizing the higher monetary amount (2500 vs 2400) and perceives the probabilities as being roughly similar. The individual does not mention probabilities in detail, nor does the report refer to skewness, distribution, or the relative size of favorable versus unfavorable outcomes. The focus is on the amount of money and the similarity of winning chances, suggesting a preference for the higher payoff with similar odds. No reference to skewness, left-skewness, or probability distribution is made in the report. The deliberation process described centers on the magnitude of the prize and the perceived similarity of probabilities, not on the shape of the probability distribution or skewness.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating skewness — specifically, whether a lottery has a greater probability assigned to the favorable (non-zero) outcome than to the unfavorable (zero) outcome. In this case, both lotteries have less than 50% probability on the favorable outcome (A: 34%, B: 33%), so neither is left-skewed. Therefore, the decision reason is not applicable. The individual’s reported reasoning focuses on the higher monetary amount and similar winning chances, not on skewness or distribution shape. There is no mention of skewness or left-skewness in the verbal report. Since the individual did not consider or apply the skewness-based rule, and the reason itself is not triggered (as no lottery is left-skewed), the decision reason was not used. The assessment is that the individual did not use the decision reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

